id: pm-swarm-4q55
title: "The identity gate now recognizes absolute home paths only at path boundaries, so prose and relative paths no longer block the GitHub issue sync"
description: "pm-swarm#29 (automated GitHub issue sync) failed identity:check on two commits. The imported issue text contains 'The exact mismatch/root cause has not been isolated', and the gate's root-home alternative (a word boundary instead of a path boundary) matched it. A false positive fails every later gate run until the commit leaves the audited range, so the sync stays blocked. The same pattern also missed Windows profiles written with doubled, JSON-escaped separators."
type: Issue
status: in_progress
priority: 1
tags[3]: ci,identity-gate,privacy
created_at: "2026-10-31T15:23:59.815Z"
updated_at: "2026-11-01T03:22:23.772Z"
assignee: claude-orchestrator
claim_principal: claude-orchestrator
author: claude-orchestrator
acceptance_criteria: "containsAbsoluteHomePath refuses absolute Linux, macOS, root-home and Windows profile paths (including doubled separators) and allows relative names and prose; both new tests fail with the old pattern; release:check passes; pm-swarm#29 passes after rebasing on the fix."
comments[10]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-10-31T15:24:03.809Z",claude-orchestrator,"Revert proof: with the previous pattern, 'home path detection refuses absolute homes and allows relative names and prose' and 'identity policy admits prose that only resembles a root home path' fail (5 pass / 2 fail); with the fix 7/7 pass. Test fixtures and this item's text build or describe home paths without writing one, so the commit passes its own gate. Same detector shape as pm-review's already-fixed gate, applied as an edit to pm-swarm's own file. The six other copies with the old pattern are tracked in the companion (pm-cli-website-hv91). Gates: 957/957 tests, coverage 100/100/100/100 over 37 files, docstrings 187."
  "2026-10-31T16:13:56.242Z",claude-orchestrator,"CodeRabbit round on 4d2e829 (thread PRRT_kwDOVLY-P86ySTG_, two Major findings, both valid). (1) The exclusion lookbehind treated any non-ASCII or punctuation character as a token boundary, so relative paths whose previous component ends in such a character (a CJK folder name, c++) were refused. (2) A file://localhost URL slipped through, because its path slash follows a letter. Fix: a positive path-start boundary (line start, a leading diff marker, whitespace, quotes, opening brackets, assignment/list separators, emphasis, file:// and file://localhost); the audit strips the patch marker before testing. Revert proof: the new corpora fail with 4d2e829's detector (first failure: the file://localhost case); a direct check shows the old pattern refusing both relative cases. 7/7 identity tests pass."
  "2026-10-31T17:20:01.323Z",claude-orchestrator,"CodeRabbit round 2 on 228412f: four Major findings in three threads, all valid. Missed: home paths glued to command options (the cc include and ld library flags) and repeated leading slashes, both regressions against the original detector. Wrongly refused: a remote URL's query value. Missed: Windows profiles in another letter case. Fix, closing the neighbors too: any single-letter option glued to the path is a boundary; one or more leading slashes; homes and the root home below a var directory (Fedora Silverblue, macOS); remote URLs of any scheme of two or more characters except file are blanked before matching; the Windows profile matches any case. The macOS Users form stays case-sensitive on purpose, because a lowercase users segment is usually a web route (corpus case: a GET route for the current user). Revert proof: the round-2 corpora fail with the 228412f detector (first failure: the include-flag case); a direct check shows it missing the include flag and the doubled slash, and refusing the redirect query value. 7/7 identity tests pass."
  "2026-10-31T18:28:13.547Z",claude-orchestrator,"CodeRabbit round 3 on 0dbcbfd: two findings, both valid. Major: URI schemes are case-insensitive, so an upper-case file scheme was masked as a remote URL and its home path slipped through. The file and localhost authorities in the path-start boundary were case-sensitive too. Minor: the remote-URL mask stopped at an opening parenthesis, a valid URL path character, so the rest of such a URL reached the detector. Fix: the remote-URL mask uses the i flag (so the file exception is case-insensitive) and ends only at a closing parenthesis, which still ends a Markdown link; the file and localhost authorities match any case. Revert proof: the round-3 corpus fails with 0dbcbfd's detector (first failure: the upper-case file URL). 7/7 identity tests pass."
  "2026-10-31T19:49:59.115Z",claude-orchestrator,"CodeRabbit round 4 on 6f57ea5: one Major finding, valid. A single-letter option glued to a home path was only a path start after a line start, whitespace, a quote or an opening bracket, so an option inside an assignment (a CFLAGS-style variable) or a list (a linker -Wl option list) was missed. The option branch now also accepts assignment and list separators before the option. Corpus: three such cases; they fail with 6f57ea5's detector (first failure: the CFLAGS case). 7/7 identity tests pass."
  "2026-10-31T21:01:32.467Z",claude-orchestrator,"CodeRabbit round 5 on c802f8b: three findings, all valid. (1) Major: a home path after a fullwidth colon was missed. Rather than adding single characters, every Unicode opening punctuation, initial quote, space separator and other punctuation now starts a path, except dot, slash and backslash, which continue a name; letters in any script still continue a name. (2) Major: in a CSV line, a remote URL followed by a comma and a separate home-path field was masked as one URL. A comma or semicolon followed by a slash now ends the URL. (3) Minor: the changelog line described the defect; the title now describes the fix. Revert proof: the round-5 corpus fails with c802f8b's detector (first failure: the fullwidth-colon case), and the old URL mask removed the CSV path. Bun evaluates the Unicode lookbehind identically. 7/7 identity tests pass."
  "2026-10-31T21:56:16.462Z",claude-orchestrator,"CodeRabbit round 6 on 64c433e: two findings, both valid. (1) Major: the remote-URL mask's unbounded scheme was retried at every word boundary, so a long scheme-like run scanned quadratically (10k characters 109 ms, 40k 1.9 s, 80k 8.7 s). The scheme is now capped at 32 characters, so each start does bounded work: 40k characters 5 ms, 400k 49 ms. The regression requires an 80k-character run to finish in under 1 s (old detector: 8.7 s). (2) Minor: the root home before a closing bracket or Unicode punctuation (an ideographic full stop) was not detected; the end boundary now accepts closing, final-quote and other punctuation and Unicode spaces, still not a dash or an underscore. 8/8 identity tests pass; both new tests fail with 64c433e's detector."
  "2026-10-31T23:28:19.842Z",claude-orchestrator,"CodeRabbit round 7 on 04dc0fc: one Major, valid, and in tension with round 5's CSV rule. A comma followed by a slash inside a URL query (a search parameter that lists paths) ended the masked URL, so the query value was refused as a local path. Resolution: the comma-or-semicolon split applies only to the authority and path. Once a query or fragment starts, commas and slashes stay inside the masked URL. A CSV line whose URL field has its own query is therefore read as one URL, as documented. Revert proof: the round-7 must-allow cases fail with 04dc0fc's mask (first failure: the search-query case); the round-5 CSV refusals still pass. The scan stays linear (400k characters in under 100 ms)."
  "2026-10-31T23:59:10.544Z",claude-orchestrator,"CodeRabbit round 8 on 9f9cc19: three findings, all valid. (1) Major: an absolute path written with a dot segment, which resolves into a home, was no longer detected (a regression against main). Closing the class, the text is now lexically normalized before matching: dot segments and repeated slashes collapse, segment/.. pairs cancel, and a leading /.. resolves to the root, in at most 64 linear passes. Relative dot-segment paths stay relative. (2) Minor: the Windows branch used an ASCII word boundary, so a drive letter after a CJK letter was refused. It now uses a Unicode letter/digit/mark/underscore lookbehind, the same rule as PATH_START. (3) Trivial, provenance: the full-gate figures in earlier comments on this item (957/957 tests, coverage, docstrings, release:check) are the orchestrator's self-reported local runs, not linked test receipts. The verification of record is CI on the PR head. The linked focused identity test was re-run with pm test --run, and its receipt is recorded with provenance. Revert proof: the round-8 corpus fails with 9f9cc19's detector (first failure: the dot-segment home case), and the old Windows branch refuses the CJK-prefixed case. 8/8 identity tests pass; the nested-chain scan stays bounded."
  "2026-11-01T03:22:23.772Z",claude-orchestrator,"Rounds 9 (CodeRabbit and cubic) on 07a9b59: four findings, all valid. A Windows path with parent or dot segments, a root-parent after a file authority or a fullwidth colon, deep nesting beyond the old 64-pass cap, and a repeated separator before a parent were all missed. Instead of another regex patch, the detector now resolves paths. It finds path tokens at a path start (optionally after a file or file-localhost authority), resolves each one in a single linear pass (empty and single-dot components drop, a parent pops one component and never goes above the root), and refuses when the resolved path names a home: a user home or Windows profile followed by a user or marked as a directory, or the root home. POSIX tokens split only on slashes, so escaped regex text that mentions a home directory stays allowed, as before; Windows tokens (a drive letter, also after a file authority) split on both separators. A colon stays inside a token, so a drive letter after a file authority is resolved, and the root home allows trailing dots or colons. identity:check over every post-baseline commit: 0 violations. Coverage of identity-gate.ts: 100% in all four dimensions. The full local coverage run's only failure is the unrelated privacy-punctuation test hitting its 120 s timeout under host load (it passed alone and on CI). Revert proof: the round-9 corpus fails with 07a9b59's detector (first failure: the Windows parent-segment case)."
files[2]{path,scope}:
  scripts/identity-gate.ts,project
  test/tooling.test.ts,project
tests[1]{command,scope,provenance{author,created_at,source_kind,source_ref}}:
  node --test --test-name-pattern='home path|identity policy' test/tooling.test.ts,project,claude-orchestrator,"2026-10-31T15:24:03.014Z",local_mutation,fix/identity-gate-path-boundary
